Here's a simple function in JavaScript that uses recursion to calculate the nth Fibonacci number:

```javascript
/**
 * Returns the nth Fibonacci number.
 * 
 * @param {number} n - The position of the Fibonacci number to return.
 * @returns {number} The nth Fibonacci number.
 */
function fibonacci(n) {
    if (n <= 0) {
        throw new Error("Input should be a positive integer.");
    } else if (n == 1) {
        return 0;
    } else if (n == 2) {
        return 1;
    } else {
        return fibonacci(n-1) + fibonacci(n-2);
    }
}

// Example usage:
console.log(fibonacci(10));  // Output: 55
```

However, this recursive approach is not efficient for large values of n because it performs many redundant calculations. We can improve this by using dynamic programming to store the results of previous calculations:

```javascript
/**
 * Returns the nth Fibonacci number.
 * 
 * @param {number} n - The position of the Fibonacci number to return.
 * @returns {number} The nth Fibonacci number.
 */
function fibonacci(n) {
    if (n <= 0) {
        throw new Error("Input should be a positive integer.");
    } else if (n == 1) {
        return 0;
    } else if (n == 2) {
        return 1;
    }

    let fib = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
    fib[1] = 0;
    fib[2] = 1;

    for (let i = 3; i <= n; i++) {
        fib[i] = fib[i-1] + fib[i-2];
    }

    return fib[n];
}

// Example usage:
console.log(fibonacci(10));  // Output: 55
```

This version of the function has a time complexity of O(n) and a space complexity of O(n), making it much more efficient for large values of n.

