The most efficient way to insert a new element into an existing unsorted array is by using the `insert()` method or the `splice()` method. Both methods have a time complexity of O(n), where n is the number of elements in the array.

However, the `splice()` method is generally more efficient because it only shifts the elements after the insertion point, whereas the `insert()` method creates a new array and copies all the elements after the insertion point to the new array.

Here's an example of how to use the `insert()` method:

```javascript
function insertElement(array, index, element) {
    array.splice(index, 0, element);
    return array;
}

const array = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
const newArray = insertElement(array, 2, 10);
console.log(newArray); // Output: [1, 2, 10, 3, 4, 5]
```

And here's an example of how to use the `splice()` method:

```javascript
function insertElement(array, index, element) {
    array.splice(index, 0, element);
    return array;
}

const array = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
const newArray = insertElement(array, 2, 10);
console.log(newArray); // Output: [1, 2, 10, 3, 4, 5]
```

It's worth noting that if you want to insert an element at a specific position in the array, you can use the `push()` method and then use the `unshift()` method to shift the elements after the insertion point:

```javascript
function insertElement(array, element) {
    array.push(element);
    return array;
}

const array = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
const newArray = insertElement(array, 10);
console.log(newArray); // Output: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 10]
```

Then, you can use the `unshift()` method to shift the elements after the insertion point:

```javascript
function insertElement(array, element) {
    array.push(element);
    return array;
}

const array = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
const newArray = insertElement(array, 10);
const shiftedArray = newArray.unshift(10);
console.log(shiftedArray); // Output: [10, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
```

However, this approach is less efficient than using the `splice()` method because it involves two operations: `push()` and `unshift()`.

