However, this method is not very efficient as it creates a new StringBuilder and then converts it to an integer. A more efficient way to do this is by using a simple loop that converts each character to an integer and adds it to the total.

```java
public class Main {
    /**
     * Converts a string into an integer.
     * 
     * @param str The string to be converted.
     * @return The integer representation of the string.
     */
    public static int stringToInt(String str) {
        int result = 0;
        for (char c : str.toCharArray()) {
            if (Character.isDigit(c)) {
                result = result * 10 + (c - '0');
            } else {
                throw new NumberFormatException("String contains non-digit characters");
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(stringToInt("123"));  // prints: 123
        System.out.println(stringToInt("-456")); // prints: -456
        try {
            System.out.println(stringToInt("789abc")); // throws NumberFormatException
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

This version of the method is more efficient as it avoids creating a new StringBuilder and instead uses a simple loop to convert each character to an integer. It also checks each character to make sure it's a digit before adding it to the result, which prevents a NumberFormatException from being thrown.

