You're looking to prevent a Denial of Service (DoS) attack by limiting the number of requests from a single IP address within a certain time frame. This is known as rate limiting.

In Spring Security, you can use the `RateLimiter` class from the `org.springframework.security.web.util` package to achieve this. Here's an example of how you can use it:

```java
import org.springframework.security.web.util.RateLimiter;
import org.springframework.security.web.util.RateLimiterConfig;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.springframework.web.filter.GenericFilterBean;

@Component
public class RateLimiterFilter extends GenericFilterBean {

    private final RateLimiterConfig rateLimiterConfig;

    public RateLimiterFilter(RateLimiterConfig rateLimiterConfig) {
        this.rateLimiterConfig = rateLimiterConfig;
    }

    @Override
    public void doFilter(ServletRequest request, ServletResponse response, FilterChain chain) throws IOException, ServletException {
        RateLimiter limiter = rateLimiterConfig.getLimiter();
        if (limiter == null) {
            return;
        }

        String ip = request.getHeader("X-Forwarded-For");
        if (ip == null) {
            ip = request.getRemoteAddr();
        }

        if (limiter.isExceeded(ip)) {
            // Handle the case where the rate limit has been exceeded
            // You can return an error response or throw an exception
            response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_TOOManyRequests);
            return;
        }

        // Allow the request to proceed
        chain.doFilter(request, response);
    }
}
```

In the above code, we're using the `RateLimiter` class to check if the rate limit has been exceeded for the current IP address. If it has, we return an error response. If not, we allow the request to proceed.

